s write some big brackets around the expression in parentheses, keeping the minus sign *outside*.\n\n$$\n15-[3(k+3)]\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the expression inside the brackets using the distributive law. This gives\n\n$$\n15-[3k+9].\n$$\n\nNow, we remove the brackets by distributing the negative sign. This gives\n\n$$\n15-3k-9.\n$$\n\nFinally, we collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15-3k-9 & = \\\\ (15-9)-3k & = \\\\ 6-3\\,k & \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475950 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186475864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475950 #edb/ref 1069 "q-141957" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475950 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475950 #edb/ref 1071 "$-5z-6(2z-3)=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475950 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "98d2da5e-a56a-51ef-b628-e972a6b524aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475950 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the expression we need to subtract from $-5z$ must be expanded using the distributive law. \n\nSo first, let's write some big brackets around the expression in parentheses, keeping the minus sign *outside*.\n\n$$\n-5z-[6(2z-3)]\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the expression inside the brackets using the distributive law. This gives\n\n$$\n-5z-[12z-18].\n$$\n\nNow, we remove the brackets by distributing the negative sign. This gives\n\n$$\n-5z-12z+18.\n$$\n\nFinally, we collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5z-12z+18 & = \\\\ (-5z-12z)+18 & = \\\\ -17z+18 & \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475951 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186475844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475951 #edb/ref 1069 "q-144243" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475951 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475951 #edb/ref 1071 "Simplify the expression $-3-(7u-5).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475951 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9aa821db-a963-5bfc-89f9-99a159ea39a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475951 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we remove the parentheses by distributing the negative sign. This gives\n\n$$\n-3-7u+5\n$$\n\nFinally, we collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3-7u+5 & = \\\\ (-3+5)-7u & = \\\\ 2-7u & \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475981 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186475849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475981 #edb/ref 1069 "q-144260" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475981 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475981 #edb/ref 1071 "$7-2(4b-3)=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475981 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "effd2690-1ce3-5bfd-837f-fb52a891061c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475981 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the expression we need to subtract from $7$ must be expanded using the distributive law. \n\nSo first, let's write some big brackets around the expression in parentheses, keeping the minus sign *outside*.\n\n$$\n7-[2(4b-3)]\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the expression inside the brackets using the distributive law. This gives\n\n$$\n7-[8b-6].\n$$\n\nNow, we remove the brackets by distributing the negative sign. This gives\n\n$$\n7-8b+6.\n$$\n\nFinally, we collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7-8b+6 & = \\\\ (7+6)-8b & = \\\\ 13-8b & \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475982 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186475839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475982 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186475841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475982 #edb/ref 1069 "q-171523" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475982 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475982 #edb/ref 1071 "The expression $-a-(3a+1)$ can be written as{{field-1}}\n$a\\,-$\n{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475982 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f10306d0-b077-5314-9baa-700a263e9595" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475982 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we remove the parentheses by distributing the negative sign. This gives\n\n$$\n-a-3a-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-a-3a-1 & = \\\\ (-a-3a)-1 & = \\\\ -4a-1 & \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475983 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186475831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475983 #edb/ref 1069 "q-144252" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475983 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475983 #edb/ref 1071 "$-13-3(2y+3)=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475983 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fbe26239-f4a5-5ec1-a88c-e3440a20795a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475983 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the expression we need to subtract from $-13$ must be expanded using the distributive law. \n\nSo first, let's write some big brackets around the expression in parentheses, keeping the minus sign *outside*.\n\n$$\n-13-[3(2y+3)]\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the expression inside the brackets using the distributive law. This gives\n\n$$\n-13-[6y+9].\n$$\n\nNow, we remove the brackets by distributing the negative sign. This gives\n\n$$\n-13-6y-9.\n$$\n\nFinally, we collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-13-6y-9 & = \\\\ (-13-9)-6y & = \\\\ -22-6y & \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
